{%- comment -%}
  Render one mount node for the React lookbook island.

  Accepts:
    lookbook - {Object} a `lookbook` metaobject drop

  Both lookbook sections render through this snippet. That is deliberate: the brief
  requires the product-page section to carry settings identical to the home-page one
  apart from the lookbook picker, and sharing the markup makes that true by
  construction rather than by remembering to copy every future change into two files.

  The payload is written as JSON into a data attribute rather than an inline <script>.
  Inline scripts need a nonce under a strict CSP and are awkward to re-render in the
  theme editor; a data attribute is just markup, so a section re-render carries its
  own configuration with it.

  Everything here is server-known. The island is never asked which products are in a
  lookbook -- it is told, and asks the Storefront API only what those products cost.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign handles = lookbook.product_handles.value
-%}

{%- if handles.size > 0 -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    `metafield_tag` renders a rich text field to HTML. Outputting the field directly
    yields its raw JSON document instead — which renders as visible braces on the
    page rather than as a paragraph.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture description_html -%}{{ lookbook.description | metafield_tag }}{%- endcapture -%}

  {%- capture lookbook_payload -%}
    {
      "title": {{ lookbook.title.value | json }},
      "description": {{ description_html | strip | json }},
      "handles": {{ handles | json }},
      "shop": {{ shop.permanent_domain | json }},
      "token": {{ settings.storefront_api_token | json }},
      "apiVersion": {{ settings.storefront_api_version | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        Market context. `localization.country.iso_code` is the country the shopper is
        actually browsing in, and is what Shopify resolves market pricing against.
        This single value is what makes per-market price and compare-at overrides
        work, so it comes from `localization` and is never hardcoded.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "country": {{ localization.country.iso_code | json }},
      "language": {{ localization.language.iso_code | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        Locale-aware URL root. On a multi-market store Shopify serves localized paths
        such as /en-jp/products/..., so a hardcoded "/products/" would drop a shopper
        out of their locale on every card click.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "rootUrl": {{ routes.root_url | json }},
      "columnsDesktop": {{ section.settings.columns_desktop | default: 4 | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        `columns_mobile` is a select, so its value is the string "1" or "2".
        Shopify requires range settings to have at least three steps, and three
        columns on a phone is not a real option -- a select is the honest control.
        `plus: 0` coerces it to a number so the React side receives a number
        rather than having to guess at the type.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "columnsMobile": {{ section.settings.columns_mobile | plus: 0 | default: 2 | json }},
      "showDescription": {{ section.settings.show_description | json }},
      "showPrice": {{ section.settings.show_price | json }},
      "showCompareAt": {{ section.settings.show_compare_at | json }},
      "showVendor": {{ section.settings.show_vendor | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        Design mode turns merchant-facing diagnostics on. Customers never see them.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "designMode": {{ request.design_mode | json }}
    }
  {%- endcapture -%}

  <div class="lookbook-mount" data-lookbook="{{ lookbook_payload | strip | escape }}">
    {%- comment -%}
      Fallback for when JavaScript does not run.

      Deliberately no product titles, images, or prices here. The brief requires
      product data to be fetched at runtime through the Storefront API, so rendering
      it from Liquid -- even only as a fallback -- would quietly work around the
      requirement. Product URLs are derivable from the handles we already hold, so a
      shopper without JavaScript still gets the lookbook's title and a working link
      per product, and nothing is served that should have come from the API.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <noscript>
      <h2 class="lookbook-fallback__heading">{{ lookbook.title.value }}</h2>
      <ul class="lookbook-fallback__list">
        {%- for handle in handles -%}
          <li>
            <a href="{{ routes.root_url }}products/{{ handle }}">
              {{ handle | replace: '-', ' ' | capitalize }}
            </a>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    </noscript>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
